  "buyer2Current": {
   "kind": "stimulus",
   "run": "buyer2",
   "data": "Here are the best action camera choices for a holiday traveller in Japan, depending on what kind of trip videos you want to make.\n\n## Best overall: **DJI Osmo Action 4**\n**Best for:** most travellers, city walking, food markets, shrines, skiing, hiking, rain.\n\n**Why it’s great for Japan**\n- Excellent low-light performance for Tokyo/Osaka night streets.\n- Strong stabilization for walking, cycling, trains, and hiking.\n- Waterproof without a case.\n- Front and rear screens are useful for selfies and vlogging.\n- Generally easier to use than a 360 camera.\n\n**Good if you want:** reliable travel footage with minimal fuss.\n\n---\n\n## Best classic action cam: **GoPro HERO12 Black**\n**Best for:** adventure activities, skiing in Hokkaido, snorkeling in Okinawa, cycling, hiking.\n\n**Pros**\n- Excellent stabilization.\n- Great accessory ecosystem.\n- Strong image quality in daylight.\n- Rugged and waterproof.\n\n**Cons**\n- Low light is not as strong as DJI Osmo Action 4.\n- Battery life can be less impressive in cold weather.\n\n**Good if you want:** the most established action camera platform.\n\n---\n\n## Best for creative travel videos: **Insta360 X4**\n**Best for:** solo travellers, temples, crowded streets, trains, skiing, walking tours.\n\n**Why it’s great**\n- Shoots 360° video, so you don’t need to aim perfectly.\n- Lets you reframe later for vertical TikTok/Reels or horizontal YouTube.\n- Invisible selfie stick effect is excellent for travel.\n- Great for capturing immersive places like Shibuya Crossing, Kyoto streets, or mountain views.\n\n**Cons**\n- Editing takes more time.\n- Low-light performance is decent but not as good as DJI Osmo Action 4.\n- More fragile lenses, so you need to be careful.\n\n**Good if you want:** unique, cinematic holiday footage and don’t mind editing.\n\n---\n\n## Best for low-light vlogging: **Insta360 Ace Pro**\n**Best for:** night markets, city walks, casual vlogging, food travel.\n\n**Pros**\n- Very good low-light performance.\n- Flip screen is useful for filming yourself.\n- Strong stabilization.\n- Easy to use.\n\n**Cons**\n- Bulkier than some action cameras.\n- Not as “classic rugged” as GoPro/DJI for heavy sports use.\n\n**Good if you want:** a travel/vlogging action camera, especially for night scenes.\n\n---\n\n## Best tiny camera: **Insta360 GO 3 / GO 3S**\n**Best for:** ultra-light travel, POV clips, casual moments, family trips.\n\n**Pros**\n- Very small and discreet.\n- Easy to mount on clothing, hats, bags, or a pendant mount.\n- Great for short travel clips.\n\n**Cons**\n- Not the best image quality compared with larger cameras.\n- Battery and storage are more limited.\n- Less ideal as your only camera for a big trip.\n\n**Good if you want:** tiny, fun, casual clips without carrying much gear.\n\n---\n\n## Best budget option: **DJI Osmo Action 3**\n**Best for:** travellers who want solid quality for less money.\n\n**Pros**\n- Good stabilization.\n- Waterproof.\n- Good battery life.\n- Often cheaper than newer models.\n\n**Cons**\n- Low-light performance is weaker than Osmo Action 4.\n- Image quality is good but not class-leading.\n\n**Good if you want:** a capable action camera without spending top-tier money.\n\n---\n\n# My top picks for Japan\n\n## If you want one camera only\n**DJI Osmo Action 4**  \nBest balance of image quality, low-light, durability, and ease of use.\n\n## If you want the most creative travel footage\n**Insta360 X4**  \nBest for unique “third-person” travel shots and reframing later.\n\n## If you will film lots at night\n**Insta360 Ace Pro** or **DJI Osmo Action 4**  \nBoth are better choices than a GoPro for night city footage.\n\n## If you mainly do adventure activities\n**GoPro HERO12 Black** or **DJI Osmo Action 4**  \nBoth are excellent for skiing, hiking, cycling, and water activities.\n\n---\n\n# Accessories worth bringing to Japan\n\n- **Extra batteries** — especially for cold areas like Hokkaido or the Japanese Alps.\n- **Fast microSD cards** — V30 or better; 128GB/256GB is ideal.\n- **Small tripod/selfie stick** — useful for shrines, city views, and solo travel.\n- **Chest or backpack strap mount** — good for walking footage.\n- **Lens protectors** — especially important for Insta360 X4.\n- **Compact power bank** — very useful for long sightseeing days.\n- **Rain cover or waterproof case** if you expect heavy rain, snow, or sea use.\n\n---\n\n# Japan-specific tips\n\n- Be respectful when filming in temples, shrines, restaurants, trains, and private shops. Some places prohibit photography or video.\n- Avoid filming people too closely without permission, especially children or in quiet public spaces.\n- Japan is very safe, but don’t leave gear unattended in cafés or train stations.\n- If buying in Japan, check:\n  - Whether the camera menu supports your language.\n  - International warranty coverage.\n  - Tax-free eligibility for tourists.\n  - Whether the price is actually better than at home.\n\n---\n\n## Quick recommendation\n\nFor most holiday travellers in Japan, I’d choose:\n\n**1. DJI Osmo Action 4** — best all-rounder  \n**2. Insta360 X4** — best creative 360 travel camera  \n**3. Insta360 Ace Pro** — best if you’ll vlog and shoot at night  \n**4. GoPro HERO12 Black** — best if you prefer the classic action camera ecosystem"
